---
/**
 * Who writes this.
 *
 * Written separately from Deep Creek's version, not adapted from it. The first
 * attempt was a find-and-replace and shared eighteen sentences with the other
 * site, which is the exact failure the voice audit flagged. Same person, two
 * different relationships to two different places, so the pages should have
 * nothing in common but the name.
 */
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

const schema = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "AboutPage",
    mainEntity: {
      "@type": "Person",
      name: "Matt Werth",
      jobTitle: "Software engineer",
      worksFor: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Werth Design", url: "https://werth.design" },
      description: "Software engineer and photographer. Travels the US to see parks, and writes and checks the Delaware Field Guide.",
    },
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Who writes this — Delaware Field Guide"
  description="Matt Werth writes and checks this site. Software engineer and photographer, north Wilmington, rings the town clerk before writing anything down."
  schema={schema}
>
  <div class="wrap pt-11 pb-2">
    <p class="label m-0">About</p>
    <h1 class="text-[2.2rem] sm:text-[2.9rem] mt-3.5 mb-0 max-w-[14ch] text-marsh">Who writes this</h1>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap prose-field pb-4">
    <p class="text-[1.2rem] leading-[1.5] font-medium max-w-[56ch]">
      Matt Werth. Software engineer, photographer, and I drive south a lot.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      I live in north Wilmington and I travel all over the US to look at parks,
      which is how I ended up caring enough about my own state to check it
      properly.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      Most of this was checked with children in the back of the car and a boot
      full of their things. That shapes it. Restrooms get more attention here
      than scenery, because on the day you need one, scenery is not the problem
      you have. The photographs are mine.
    </p>

    <h2>It started with the dog</h2>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      I wanted to know which beach the dog could walk on. I got three answers.
      Two were out of date.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      Rehoboth had moved its dates in January 2026 and most of the internet
      hadn't caught up. That isn't carelessness. The city published the change
      properly. It's that nobody goes back to a page they wrote three years ago,
      and almost nobody wrote down where the fact came from, so there's no way
      to tell a fresh answer from a stale one by looking at it.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      Once I started checking I found two more of the same shape. State park
      entry fees roughly doubled in March 2026. Seven surf fishing crossings
      dropped their reservation requirement in 2025. Both properly announced.
      Both still printed the old way in plenty of places.
    </p>

    <h2>What I do about it</h2>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      I ring whoever sets the rule. For a beach ordinance that's the city clerk.
      For a state park it's DNREC. For a county park it's the county. Then I
      write down the date I asked and the name of who answered, and both go on
      the page.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      Where I've only read something off an official page rather than asking a
      person, the page says that instead. They're different claims and you
      should get to see which one you're holding.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      Anything I haven't checked is marked unchecked. It makes the site look
      thinner than the guides it competes with, which is fine.
    </p>

    <h2>The day job</h2>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      The engineering is why this is mostly tables and dates rather than travel
      writing. I run <a href="https://werth.design" rel="noopener">Werth
      Design</a> in Wilmington, and it pays for this. Nothing here is sponsored
      at the moment. If a page ever earns anything, that page will tell you.
    </p>

    <h2>Corrections</h2>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      When I get something wrong I leave the mistake up with the date and what
      it used to say. <a href="/changed/">There's a log of what's changed</a>.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      If you spot one, the report box at the foot of every page takes about ten
      seconds. People who live in these towns know them better than I do, and a
      note from someone standing there yesterday is worth more to me than
      another phone call.
    </p>

    <h2>Related</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/how-we-verify/">How I check things</a></li>
      <li><a href="/changed/">What's changed, and when</a></li>
      <li><a href="/verified/">Every fact on the site, with its source and age</a></li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</Layout>
